---

I need this word back. Here's what's wrong with it and what I mean when I use it.

:::divider

## A word carrying centuries it didn't start with

The Greek word *psych&#275;* &mdash; which the New Testament uses roughly a hundred times &mdash; did not mean what most English readers think it means when they read &ldquo;soul&rdquo; in their Bibles.

They think: immortal substance. A ghost that survives the death of the body, escapes to heaven, and that&rsquo;s the point of the whole story.

That&rsquo;s Plato. Not Paul.

The Hebrews had a different word entirely, and the Greeks themselves used *psych&#275;* in ways the Christian tradition quietly narrowed over time. If I&rsquo;m going to use &ldquo;soul&rdquo; in my own writing &mdash; and I am &mdash; I owe you a clear account of what I&rsquo;m actually reaching for.

## What the Hebrews meant

The Hebrew word *nephesh* appears roughly 750 times in the Old Testament. It does not mean an immortal inner passenger. It means: throat. Neck. Appetite. The whole living creature in its desire and vulnerability.

When Genesis says God formed Adam and he became a &ldquo;living *nephesh*&rdquo; (Gen 2:7), it doesn&rsquo;t mean God installed an immortal soul into a clay body and the soul will escape when the clay wears out. It means: the dust animated by God&rsquo;s breath became *a living creature*. The whole thing. Animals are called *nephesh* too. The *nephesh* dies. It can be killed. It is not a ghost &mdash; it is you, the whole you, as a living being.

The psalmist says &ldquo;my *nephesh* thirsts for God.&rdquo; Not a part of him. His whole being. His throat. What he craves at the level of life itself.

The New Testament word *psych&#275;* was the translation choice for *nephesh* in the Septuagint, and Paul and the gospel writers inherited that choice. But *psych&#275;* had its own history &mdash; and that history already had a different shape from *nephesh*.

## What the Greeks meant

Before the New Testament writers got hold of it, *psych&#275;* in Greek philosophy had already been pulling toward the immortal-substance reading. Plato&rsquo;s *Phaedo* argues for an immortal soul that is trapped in the body, purified by philosophy, and released at death to return to the realm of Forms. That reading is everywhere in Western culture. It&rsquo;s how most English speakers have heard the word &ldquo;soul&rdquo; their whole lives.

But the New Testament does not consistently use *psych&#275;* that way. When Jesus says &ldquo;whoever wants to save his *psych&#275;* will lose it&rdquo; (Mark 8:35), he does not mean &ldquo;whoever wants to save his immortal passenger should be careful.&rdquo; He means: whoever wants to preserve his life &mdash; his whole self, his existence, his being &mdash; will lose it. It&rsquo;s closer to *nephesh* than to Plato.

Paul distinguishes *psych&#275;* from *pneuma* (spirit) and *nous* (mind). The *psychikos* person (1 Cor 2:14) is not the person who has a soul. It&rsquo;s the person who lives at the level of natural life, unaware of the Spirit&rsquo;s presence. The *pneumatikos* person is the one whose whole being &mdash; soul, spirit, mind, body &mdash; is being opened to participation in God.

So already in the New Testament itself, the word &ldquo;soul&rdquo; is doing different work than what the reader hears. The translation tradition flattened the distinctions. And then the theological tradition layered more on top.

## What my tradition did with it

Origen read *psych&#275;* through a different lens. For him, the soul is not a substance made by God out of nothing in the usual sense. It is a pre-existent reality that fell from contemplation of the Logos, cooled into its current state (*psych&#275;* from *psychesthai*, he speculated &mdash; &ldquo;to grow cold&rdquo;), and is now being restored by remembering what it was before the fall. This is not Platonist immortality. It&rsquo;s procession-and-return: the soul comes from the Logos and returns through recognition.

Augustine read the soul as the image of God &mdash; the inner self, the place of encounter, the dimension of the person that can know and be known by God. His formula: &ldquo;Return to yourself. Transcend yourself. Go beyond yourself. The truth dwells within.&rdquo;

Eckhart went further. The *F&uuml;nklein* &mdash; the &ldquo;little spark&rdquo; in the soul &mdash; is not created, he said. It is uncreated. Not a part of the soul that was made by God. It is God itself, present at the bottom of the soul. The soul does not reach God across a distance. It discovers that God was already its own deepest life.

The Eastern tradition &mdash; the Cappadocians, Maximus, Palamas &mdash; developed the vocabulary of *theosis*. The soul does not escape the body to be with God. The whole person &mdash; soul, body, spirit &mdash; is transformed by participation in the divine energies. The soul remains what it is &mdash; a genuine localized perspective &mdash; while participating ever more fully in the life it never actually left.

All of these thinkers are working with the word &ldquo;soul.&rdquo; None of them mean what the average English reader hears.

## What I mean

When I say &ldquo;soul,&rdquo; I mean: the localized perspective. The bounded, first-person center of awareness that you experience as &ldquo;you.&rdquo; It is not a ghost in a machine. It is not a substance that will survive death by its own nature. It is not a part of you that escapes the rest.

It is *you* &mdash; the whole you &mdash; as a perspective within universal consciousness.

The soul is genuinely constrained by the render. You experience finitude, brain chemistry, physics, suffering, mortality. These are real. The soul is not the author of reality. It is inside the render, subject to its laws.

But the soul can also remember. The amnesia is not absolute. The soul can recognize that its own deepest life is God, localized. That recognition is what the tradition calls *theosis*. Remembering does not dissolve the soul into God &mdash; the soul that doesn&rsquo;t forget is still a soul. The railing holds.

The soul is not immortal in the Platonic sense. But in a consciousness-first universe, death is not the end of awareness. The perspective may dissolve. What it was made of &mdash; consciousness &mdash; does not. And the resurrection claim &mdash; which I hold as an honest open question &mdash; is that the localized perspective itself is not lost.

## What this changes

For a long time I used the word &ldquo;alter&rdquo; for this &mdash; borrowing from Bernardo Kastrup&rsquo;s analytic idealism, where a dissociated alter is a bounded perspective within mind at large. It&rsquo;s precise in his system. It was doing the work in mine. But it&rsquo;s Kastrup&rsquo;s word, not mine. It never warmed up. It always sounded like I was explaining a philosophical model rather than inhabiting a tradition.

&ldquo;Soul&rdquo; is the tradition&rsquo;s word. It&rsquo;s wrecked by misuse, yes. But the right response to a wreck isn&rsquo;t to abandon the word. It&rsquo;s to do the recovery work &mdash; show where the wreckage came from, clear it, and say what you actually mean.

That&rsquo;s what I&rsquo;ve just done.

Going forward, when you see &ldquo;soul&rdquo; on this site, this is what it means: the whole you, in your finitude and your infinite depth, subject to the render and participant in God. Forgetting and remembering. Bounded and open. The throat that craves, and the life that is already present as its own deepest ground.

:::divider

:::colophon
<span class="sig">Terms</span> &mdash; *Nephesh* (Hebrew): the whole living creature, the life itself, the throat/appetite &middot; *Psych&#275;* (Greek): soul, life, self &mdash; carries different weight in Plato vs. the NT &middot; *Pneuma* (Greek): spirit, breath, the animating dimension; distinguished from psych&#275; by Paul &middot; *Nous* (Greek): mind, intellect; the mode of knowing that participates in the Logos &middot; *Theosis*: deification &mdash; the soul&rsquo;s transformation by participation in the divine life

For the full three-level ontology (Ground, render, soul) and the amnesia&#8596;remembering axis, see [The Logos lineage](/posts/the-logos-lineage.html).
:::end
